#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T$.\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T$.\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21}$, we obtain $R \\neq R^T$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161751 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n2. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n3. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n$A = A^T$\n\nEquivalently,\n\n$$\na_{ij} = a_{ji}\n$$\n\nfor all $i,j$. In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A$, we have\n$(A + A^{T})^{T}|= A^{T} + (A^{T})^{T}; = A^{T} + A; = A + A^{T}$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n$AB|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be50ba1-87ed-5e49-bb3d-1a6da1c3686d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160705} {:answer/id #uuid "d45127db-5388-5643-94dd-467ae055143c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160706} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2f65fb-64fb-5493-b427-86dee9cec2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160707} {:answer/id #uuid "431c09e0-a899-57af-bee3-4c677834790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160708} {:answer/id #uuid "54ec641a-3d94-5b8a-a8f1-4a8b01f21fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "431c09e0-a899-57af-bee3-4c677834790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160708} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ac9f5a-177e-5127-b3dc-d2e82a45d0f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160704}] :question/id #uuid "dd69c0a4-182b-55d3-9077-6d410397ebbd" :question/ma